::JOUR_U01_L02_Q01::MLA.JOUR.ETH.02
A newsroom team disagrees about a headline. Which response best supports ethical collaboration? {
~Let the person with the strongest opinion choose immediately.#Incorrect. Ethical collaboration requires more than volume or pressure.
~Ignore the disagreement and publish both headlines.#Incorrect. Publishing both may confuse the audience and avoid the problem.
=Discuss the concern, compare the headline to the facts, and revise together.#Correct. This protects accuracy and respectful compromise.
~Cancel the story because disagreement occurred.#Incorrect. Disagreement can be solved through a responsible process.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q02::MLA.JOUR.INT.01
Which sentence best shows active listening? {
~You are wrong, and we are doing my idea.#Incorrect. This shuts down collaboration.
=I hear that you are concerned about the quote being unchecked, so let's verify it before final review.#Correct. This restates the concern and moves toward a solution.
~We do not have time to talk, so stop bringing it up.#Incorrect. This dismisses a teammate's concern.
~I already know what you mean, so I do not need to listen.#Incorrect. This is not active listening.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q03::MLA.JOUR.REP.05
Which plan best shows time management in a newsroom workflow? {
~Everyone waits until publication time to choose tasks.#Incorrect. This increases the chance of missed deadlines.
=Reporter verifies the quote by 10:00, editor reviews by 10:20, and designer updates by 10:35.#Correct. This plan has clear roles and checkpoints.
~The team skips editing because the story is almost finished.#Incorrect. Skipping editing can weaken accuracy and quality.
~One teammate completes every task while the others watch.#Incorrect. This does not use collaborative workflow well.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q04::MLA.JOUR.ETH.02
What does respectful compromise mean in a journalism team? {
~Giving up accuracy so everyone feels finished#Incorrect. A compromise should not remove accuracy.
~Letting the fastest teammate decide every issue#Incorrect. Speed alone is not respectful collaboration.
~Avoiding all disagreement by not sharing concerns#Incorrect. Silence does not solve production problems.
=Adjusting a plan while protecting accuracy, fairness, and team roles#Correct. Good compromise solves a problem without weakening quality.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q05::MLA.JOUR.ETH.02
Which situation shows collaborative media production? {
=A reporter, editor, and designer divide tasks, review accuracy, and agree on a final layout.#Correct. The team works toward a cohesive publication.
~A writer publishes without showing the work to anyone.#Incorrect. This does not show collaboration.
~A designer changes facts to fit a graphic.#Incorrect. Changing facts harms accuracy.
~A teammate ignores all deadlines because the idea is creative.#Incorrect. Time management is part of collaborative production.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q06::MLA.JOUR.INT.01
In a meeting, one teammate says the graphic date may be wrong. What should the team do first? {
~Say the graphic looks good enough and move on.#Incorrect. The concern may affect accuracy.
~Delete the graphic without checking anything.#Incorrect. This may be unnecessary and skips discussion.
=Listen to the concern and compare the graphic date with the verified information.#Correct. Active listening and checking facts address the problem.
~Blame the designer for slowing the team down.#Incorrect. Blame weakens collaboration and does not solve the issue.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q07::MLA.JOUR.REP.05
Why should a newsroom team assign roles? {
~Roles make it impossible to help each other.#Incorrect. Clear roles can still allow support.
~Roles remove the need for deadlines.#Incorrect. Roles and deadlines work together.
=Roles clarify responsibility and reduce missed tasks.#Correct. Defined roles support time management and quality.
~Roles let the team skip fact checking.#Incorrect. Fact checking remains necessary.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q08::MLA.JOUR.ETH.02
Which choice is an unethical collaboration shortcut? {
~Asking a teammate to clarify a concern#Incorrect. Clarifying a concern supports collaboration.
~Dividing tasks so the deadline can be met#Incorrect. Task division can be responsible when accuracy is protected.
~Reviewing the final publication before submitting#Incorrect. Final review supports quality.
=Publishing a quote that no one has verified because the team is tired#Correct. This is unethical because it sacrifices accuracy.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q09::MLA.JOUR.INT.01
Which behavior weakens active listening? {
~Taking notes while a teammate explains a concern#Incorrect. Notes can support active listening.
=Planning your reply while ignoring the speaker's evidence#Correct. This prevents real listening.
~Asking a clarifying question#Incorrect. Clarifying questions support listening.
~Restating the speaker's main point#Incorrect. Restating shows understanding.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q10::MLA.JOUR.ETH.02
A teammate suggests a dramatic headline that is not supported by the story. What should the editor do? {
~Use it because dramatic language always improves journalism.#Incorrect. Drama does not justify an unsupported claim.
=Explain the accuracy issue and help revise the headline to match the evidence.#Correct. This protects accuracy and supports respectful compromise.
~Remove the teammate from the project without discussion.#Incorrect. This skips collaborative problem solving.
~Publish the headline and hope readers do not notice.#Incorrect. Hoping errors are unnoticed is not ethical journalism.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q11::MLA.JOUR.REP.05
Which tool would best help a team track production workflow? {
~A blank page with no task list#Incorrect. A workflow needs visible tasks or checkpoints.
~A private plan only one teammate can see#Incorrect. A team workflow should be shared.
=A shared checklist with roles, deadlines, and final review steps#Correct. This supports time management and accountability.
~A list of ideas with no due dates#Incorrect. Ideas alone do not manage production time.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q12::MLA.JOUR.ETH.02
Which outcome best shows a cohesive collaborative publication? {
~Each section contradicts the others.#Incorrect. Contradiction weakens cohesiveness.
~The design hides important facts.#Incorrect. Design should support information.
~The team publishes separate unfinished pieces.#Incorrect. Unfinished pieces do not create a cohesive product.
=The story, headline, graphic, and caption all support the same verified information.#Correct. Cohesiveness means the parts work together accurately.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q13::MLA.JOUR.INT.01
Which question best supports collaborative discussion? {
=Can you explain which detail you think still needs verification?#Correct. This invites clarification and focuses on the work.
~Why are you always slowing us down?#Incorrect. This attacks the person rather than addressing the issue.
~Can we ignore your concern until after publication?#Incorrect. Accuracy concerns should be handled before publication.
~Would you stop talking so we can finish?#Incorrect. This dismisses collaboration.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q14::MLA.JOUR.REP.05
What is the purpose of a final review checkpoint? {
~To add random details at the last second#Incorrect. Final review should confirm quality, not add random information.
~To remove all team roles#Incorrect. Roles remain useful through final review.
=To check accuracy, completion, and format before publication#Correct. Final review protects the quality of the publication.
~To make the story less clear#Incorrect. Final review should improve clarity.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q15::MLA.JOUR.ETH.02
Which team response best balances speed and accuracy? {
~Publish now and correct errors only if someone complains.#Incorrect. Accuracy should be protected before publication.
=Set a short verification deadline, continue editing other parts, and review before publishing.#Correct. This uses time well while protecting accuracy.
~Stop all work until the next week even though one fact can be checked quickly.#Incorrect. This may not manage time effectively.
~Remove the fact checker because checking takes time.#Incorrect. Fact checking is part of quality journalism.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q16::MLA.JOUR.INT.01
Which statement would most likely reduce conflict in a newsroom discussion? {
~Your part is bad, so I fixed it without telling you.#Incorrect. This is disrespectful and skips collaboration.
~Nobody cares about your concern.#Incorrect. This dismisses a teammate.
=I understand your goal is reader interest; let's make sure the wording also matches the facts.#Correct. This acknowledges the teammate's goal and protects accuracy.
~We should ignore the editor because editing is annoying.#Incorrect. This weakens workflow and quality.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q17::MLA.JOUR.ETH.02
What is a key sign of respectful compromise? {
~One person wins and everyone else is ignored.#Incorrect. That is not compromise.
~The group removes all standards to avoid conflict.#Incorrect. Quality standards must remain.
~The team hides problems from the audience.#Incorrect. Hiding problems weakens trust.
=The team adjusts the plan while keeping the publication accurate and complete.#Correct. This is responsible compromise.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q18::MLA.JOUR.REP.05
Which missed step most directly creates a deadline risk? {
~Using a clear role list#Incorrect. Role lists reduce deadline risk.
~Checking a quote before final review#Incorrect. Verification supports quality.
=Starting layout before anyone confirms who is responsible for each task#Correct. Unclear responsibility can delay or duplicate work.
~Creating a final review time#Incorrect. Final review time helps manage the deadline.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q19::MLA.JOUR.ETH.02
Why is ethical teamwork important to audience trust? {
~The audience never sees the final product.#Incorrect. The audience sees the result of the team's process.
~Teamwork lets journalists avoid accuracy.#Incorrect. Ethical teamwork strengthens accuracy.
=The team's choices affect whether the final publication is accurate, fair, and organized.#Correct. Audience trust depends on the quality of the published work.
~A team can publish anything if everyone agrees.#Incorrect. Agreement does not replace ethical standards.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q20::MLA.JOUR.INT.01
A teammate says, "I am worried the caption names the wrong student." Which response is best? {
~It probably does not matter because the photo looks good.#Incorrect. Identification errors matter.
=Let's check the roster or source notes before the final version.#Correct. This listens to the concern and moves toward verification.
~Do not worry about names in journalism.#Incorrect. Names are important factual details.
~You should have said that after publication.#Incorrect. Concerns should be addressed before publication when possible.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q21::MLA.JOUR.REP.05
Which workflow order is strongest for a short team article? {
=Report facts, draft the piece, check accuracy, edit, then complete final review.#Correct. This order moves from information gathering to publication readiness.
~Publish first, then decide whether facts are correct.#Incorrect. Accuracy should be checked before publication.
~Design the page, invent facts, then ask questions later.#Incorrect. Facts must be reported and verified.
~Choose a headline, ignore the draft, and skip review.#Incorrect. This does not support quality production.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q22::MLA.JOUR.ETH.02
Which example shows poor collaborative ethics? {
~A team member asks for clarification about a source.#Incorrect. This is a responsible action.
~An editor checks whether the headline matches the story.#Incorrect. This protects accuracy.
~A designer updates a graphic after the date is verified.#Incorrect. This supports quality publication.
=A reporter changes a teammate's quote to make it sound stronger without checking.#Correct. This is unethical because it alters source information without verification.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q23::MLA.JOUR.INT.01
Which phrase is most respectful during disagreement? {
~Your idea makes no sense.#Incorrect. This attacks the person and weakens collaboration.
~I am ignoring this because I disagree.#Incorrect. Ignoring disagreement is not active listening.
=I see the benefit of your idea, and I think we need to check one fact before using it.#Correct. This is respectful and focused on accuracy.
~Only my version should be published.#Incorrect. This rejects collaboration.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q24::MLA.JOUR.REP.05
What should a team do if one task is behind schedule? {
~Hide the delay until publication time.#Incorrect. Hiding delays makes workflow problems worse.
~Abandon accuracy to save time.#Incorrect. Accuracy must remain protected.
=Reassign or support the task, set a short checkpoint, and update the workflow.#Correct. This manages time while keeping quality standards.
~Stop all other completed work permanently.#Incorrect. The team should solve the delay, not abandon progress.
}

::JOUR_U01_L02_Q25::MLA.JOUR.ETH.02
Which checklist best fits Lesson 2 mastery? {
~Rush, ignore concerns, publish, and hope.#Incorrect. This weakens ethics and accuracy.
=Listen, define roles, compromise respectfully, manage deadlines, and review accuracy.#Correct. This captures ethical newsroom collaboration.
~Let one person control the team and skip final review.#Incorrect. This is not collaborative or responsible.
~Choose the prettiest layout and ignore the facts.#Incorrect. Design cannot replace accuracy.
}
